\section{Introduction (with Literature Review)}

\subsection{Background}

Randomized Controlled Trials (RCTs) remain the gold standard for evaluating the efficacy and safety of novel therapeutic interventions. 
However, the clinical development sector is increasingly constrained by rising operational costs, strict time limitations, and complex 
ethical considerations \citep{dimasi_innovation_2016}. For instance, recruiting a sufficient number of patients, particularly in case of 
rare diseases, poses a major obstacle to the development of new treatments. Furthermore, randomizing patients to a placebo or active 
control arm when other effective treatments already exist raises significant ethical concerns regarding patient welfare \citep{freedman_scientific_1987, world_medical_association_world_2025}.

Historically, the dominant paradigm in the design of RCTs has been the frequentist approach. This framework evaluates long-run operating 
characteristics, relying heavily on strict Type I Error control. A fundamental limitation of this paradigm is that it treats every trial 
in isolation, ignoring pre-existing information such as data from previous clinical phases or historical control arms that could otherwise 
help the current study. 

Conversely, Bayesian statistical frameworks offer a mathematically rigorous solution to this challenge by incorporating external evidence 
through informative prior distributions. Integrating background knowledge during the planning phase can lead to highly efficient designs, 
accelerating trial execution, reducing operational waste, and limiting the number of concurrent patients required for the control arm.

This not only decreases the overall sample size but also makes the trial more attractive to potential participants, who face a lower 
probability of being assigned to a placebo group. As noted by O'Hagan et al., \textit{"the design of a trial 
is ultimately the sponsor’s decision and its outcome is the sponsor’s risk; therefore, the design should incorporate all available information to 
maximize the probability of a satisfactory outcome"} \citep{ohagan_assurance_2005}.
Recognizing these practical benefits, regulatory bodies are increasingly supportive of these methods, as 
evidenced by recent guidance on the use of Bayesian methodology in clinical trials \citep{fda_bayesian_2026}.

 

\subsection{Bayesian Designs and Operating Characteristics}
The Bayesian framework formally incorporates uncertainty into the design through prior distributions, that can be placed either directly 
on the treatment effect or on one (or both) arms:

\begin{itemize}
        \item \textbf{Borrowing on the Control Group:} when recruiting patients for a standard-of-care or placebo arm is ethically challenging or 
    operationally slow, historical data can be used to inform the control parameters. Designs that supplement a smaller concurrent control group 
    with historical information are formally referred to in the literature as hybrid-control trials. (e.g. \cite{fu2023covariate}, \cite{kopp2024simulating}, \cite{li2022hybrid}) 


    \item \textbf{Borrowing on the Treatment Effect:} When the primary goal is to use existing efficacy data to support a new indication or 
    demographic, such as leveraging adult data to pediatric cohorts, the prior can be placed on the treatment contrast directly \citep{psioda_bayesian_2019}.
\end{itemize}




Evaluating the operating characteristics of Bayesian designs requires particular attention. Traditional frequentist designs rely on 
conditional power, estimating the probability of success assuming a single, fixed treatment effect. The Bayesian framework shifts instead the focus 
to unconditional power, or Assurance, which evaluates the expected power averaged over a prior distribution for the unknown treatment effect.
Integrating over a prior provides a more realistic assessment of the predictive probability of achieving a 
statistically significant result \citep{spiegelhalter1993bayesian, ohagan_assurance_2005}. Alternative names are probability of study success (Wang et al., 2013) 
and Bayesian predictive power (Spiegelhalter, Freedman and Blackburn, 1986). 

Despite these fundamentally different philosophies regarding power, it remains common practice to evaluate Bayesian designs with informative priors 
using the classical, frequentist Type I Error rate. However, when Type I Error is defined traditionally, conditioning solely on the sampling distribution 
of the current trial's data, it is mathematically impossible to strictly control it if external information is being borrowed 
\citep{viele_use_2014, psioda_bayesian_2019, kopp-schneider_power_2020}, and a consensus on alternative metrics has yet to be reached in the literature. 


\subsection{Dynamic Borrowing and Prior-Data Conflict}
\label{subsec:info-borrowing}

While borrowing historical data presents clear advantages, it is not without risk. Historical and current trial data may be heterogeneous
due to shifts in demographics, standard of care, or measurement techniques \citep{evans_checking_2006}. If a model forces the combination
of historical and concurrent data when such a discrepancy exists, it can lead to biased estimates and inflated error rates.

Recognizing these dangers, \citet{pocock_combination_1976} proposed the first formal criteria for combining historical and concurrent
controls in a randomized design: the historical trial should be comparable in patient population, treatment regimen, and endpoint
definition, and conducted under sufficiently similar conditions. Several Bayesian frameworks have since been developed to achieve a more
principled form of discounting, in which historical information is downweighted in proportion to heterogeneity. Examples include
Power Priors \citep{ibrahim_power_2000}, which discount historical data via a scalar power parameter $a_0 \in [0,1]$, and Commensurate
Priors \citep{hobbs_2011}, which link historical and current parameters through a shared commensurability model. The common goal of all
the dynamic borrowing method is to borrow strength when historical data align with the current trial
while discounting when a true discrepancy, commonly called drift \citep{viele_use_2014}, is present.

Among these, the Meta-Analytic-Predictive (MAP) approach \citep{neuenschwander2010summarizing} derives the prior for the control arm
from a hierarchical meta-analysis of historical trials, treating the trial-specific control parameters as exchangeable. The MAP prior is
not available in closed form but can be approximated by a mixture of conjugate distributions by minimizing
$\mathrm{KL}(\pi_{\text{MAP}} \| q)$, the KL divergence of the mixture $q$ from the true predictive $\pi_{\text{MAP}}$ obtained using MCMC. However, standard MAP can still react too slowly when historical and current data disagree;  
therefore it is possible to augment the mixture with a weakly informative
component whose weight $\omega$ encodes the prior probability that the new trial is not exchangeable with the historical controls.
This Robust MAP prior responds more rapidly to prior-data conflict, shifting posterior weight toward the vague component as
divergence between historical and current data grows. This thesis focuses primarily on the MAP framework and its robust extension,
as recently illustrated in clinical practice (e.g. \cite{richeldi2022trial, chen2024efficacy}).


\subsection{Dual Criterion}
\label{subsec:dual_criterion_intro}

A limitation of standard trial designs is that success is defined by a single statistical threshold: a significant $p$-value or an equivalent posterior probability. While this guarantees sufficient evidence against the null hypothesis, 
it says nothing about whether the effect is large enough to be clinically meaningful, since a statistically significant result can arise from a modest, practically unimportant treatment difference.

\citet{roychoudhury_beyond_2018} address this through the dual-criterion design, in which trial success requires both a statistical criterion and a clinical criterion: the estimated effect must reach a pre-specified Decision Value (DV), 
the minimum clinically meaningful improvement elicited from domain experts. Each trial then has four possible outcomes: GO, NO-GO, or two inconclusive cases when only one criterion is met, while power at the DV is approximately $50\%$ 
by construction, meaning the sample size must be chosen to achieve adequate power for effects meaningfully above the DV. The Bayesian counterpart of this design appears in \citet{gsponer_practical_2014}, who require simultaneously a 
high posterior probability that the treatment effect is positive and a posterior probability of at least $50\%$ that it exceeds the clinical threshold, noting that this double criterion directly addresses scenarios where a marginally 
significant result is driven by chance rather than a genuinely large effect.




