\section{Methodology}
For the remainder of the manuscript, we focus on two-sample clinical trials with a continuous Normal endpoint and known standard deviations $\sigma_C$ and $\sigma_T$. 
Let $\theta_T$ and $\theta_C$ denote the true parameters of the treatment and control groups, respectively. The treatment contrast is defined as $\delta = \theta_C - \theta_T$,
where we assume without loss of generality that lower values of $\theta$ represent a better clinical response, thus $\delta > 0$ indicates a treatment benefit. 

We evaluate the one-sided hypothesis test:
$$
H_0: \delta \le q \quad \text{versus} \quad H_1: \delta > q
$$

where $q$ denotes the clinically meaningful threshold or non-inferiority margin.

In the Bayesian framework, the trial design evaluates this hypothesis using a posterior decision rule.
Let $\mathcal{D} = (\bar{y}_T, \bar{y}_C)$ denote the observed trial data.
The trial is declared a success (i.e., $H_0$ is rejected) if the posterior probability of $\delta$ exceeding $q$ surpasses a pre-specified threshold $p$ (typically $p = 1 - \alpha$):
\begin{equation}
    \label{eq:an_prior}
    \Pr(\delta > q \mid \mathcal{D},\, \pi_A) \ge p
\end{equation}

where $\pi_A$ explicitly denotes the analysis prior used to derive the posterior distribution.

\paragraph{Running example} 
To fix ideas, throughout this section we illustrate all results with a two-arm trial: $n_T = 40$ treatment patients, $n_C = 20$ control patients, and known standard deviation $\sigma = 88$ (lower values are better). 
The analysis prior on $\theta_C$ is a Normal with mean $\mu_{A,C} = -50$ and effective sample size $n_A = 20$ (so $\sigma_{A,C}^2 = 88^2/20$); the sampling variances are therefore $v_C = 88^2/20$ and $v_T = 88^2/40$. The decision rule is $\Pr(\delta > 0 \mid \mathcal{D},\,\pi_A) \ge 0.95$. These prior parameters are derived from a set of historical studies via the MAP approach; the historical data, the full trial design, and all operating characteristics are detailed in Section~\ref{sec:application1}. Throughout this section the prior is treated as a simple Normal; the full analysis using the MAP mixture and its robust extensions is carried out in Section~\ref{sec:application1}.

\subsection{Formulating the MAP Prior}
\label{subsec:formulating_priors}

The Meta-Analytic-Predictive (MAP) prior is derived from a hierarchical model that uses the exchangeability assumption across
historical trials \citep{schmidli_robust_2014}. Let $H$ denote the number of historical studies, with observed
control mean $\bar{y}_{h}$ and known sampling variances $v_h = \sigma^2/n_h$ for $h = 1,\ldots,H$. The model is:
\begin{align*}
    \bar{y}_h \mid \theta_h &\sim \mathcal{N}(\theta_h,\, v_h) \\
    \theta_h,\, \theta_C \mid \mu, \tau &\sim \mathcal{N}(\mu,\, \tau^2)
\end{align*}
with hyperpriors $p(\mu)$ and $p(\tau)$ placed on the population mean and between-study standard deviation. The heterogeneity
parameter $\tau$ governs the degree of borrowing: $\tau \approx 0$ implies all studies share a common control mean (full pooling),
while large $\tau$ signals large between-trial variation and justifies reduced borrowing.

The MAP prior for the new trial's control parameter $\theta_C$ is the marginal posterior predictive distribution given the historical data:
\begin{equation*} 
p\!\left(\theta_C \mid \bar{y}_1,\ldots,\bar{y}_H\right)
    \propto \int\!\int p\!\left(\theta_C \mid \mu,\tau^2\right)
    \left[\prod_{h=1}^{H} \int p(\bar{y}_h \mid \theta_h)\, p(\theta_h \mid \mu,\tau^2)\, d\theta_h\right]
    p(\mu)\,p(\tau^2)\; d\mu\; d\tau^2
\end{equation*}
This integral is not available in closed form and is evaluated via MCMC. The resulting posterior samples of $\theta_C$ are
then used to fit a $K$-component mixture of Normal distributions,
\begin{equation*}
    \pi_{\mathrm{MAP}}(\theta_C) = \sum_{k=1}^{K} w_k\,\mathcal{N}(\theta_C;\,\mu_k,\,\sigma_k^2)
\end{equation*}
chosen by minimizing the KL divergence of the mixture from the true MAP predictive \citep{schmidli_robust_2014}. This conjugate mixture
approximation enables closed-form Bayesian updating: given the current trial mean $\bar{y}_C \sim \mathcal{N}(\theta_C, v_C)$, each
Normal component is updated independently via the standard Normal-Normal formula, and the posterior is again a Normal mixture with weights proportional
to $w_k \cdot \mathcal{N}(\bar{y}_C;\, \mu_k,\, \sigma_k^2 + v_C)$ \citep{neuenschwander2010summarizing}. \note{CHECK THIS}

The Robust MAP prior augments $\pi_{\mathrm{MAP}}$ with a weakly informative component to protect against prior-data conflict:
\begin{equation*}
    \pi_{\mathrm{Robust}}(\theta_C)
    = (1 - \omega)\,\pi_{\mathrm{MAP}}(\theta_C) + \omega\,\mathcal{N}(\theta_C;\,m_R,\,\sigma_R^2),
\end{equation*}
where $\omega \in [0,1]$ is the prior probability that the new trial is not exchangeable with the historical controls, and $m_R$,
$\sigma_R^2$ are the location and variance of the vague component. Under prior-data conflict the posterior weight on the vague component
increases, limiting the influence of misleading historical information. The tuning of $\omega$, $m_R$ and $\sigma_R^2$ is not trivial:
\citet{ratta2026interplay} show that Lindley's paradox (whereby a diffuse vague component places so little mass near the null that the Bayes factor paradoxically 
favours $H_0$ even when the frequentist $p$-value is significant) and asymptotic Type I Error inflation can be avoided only when $\omega$ and $\sigma_R^2$
are calibrated jointly rather than independently. \citet{weru_information_2026} further demonstrate that replacing the vague Normal
component with a heavier-tailed form (e.g.\ a Student-$t$) or adopting a data-dependent location (Empirical Bayes) can more
effectively cap Type I Error rates and estimation bias under severe drift. Regarding $\omega$ itself, \citet{neuenschwander_fixed_2023}
argue that it need not be assigned a hyperprior: even a fixed weight is implicitly updated by the current data through the mixture
posterior weights, so a Beta prior on $\omega$ adds little beyond additional specification complexity.
Throughout this manuscript we set $m_R = -50$ (the historical mean) and $\sigma_R^2 = \sigma^2 = 88^2$, corresponding to a unit-information prior (UIP) with effective sample size of one, the standard default recommended by \citet{schmidli_robust_2014}.

\paragraph{Running Example}
To obtain the MAP prior, we rely on the R package \texttt{RBesT}\citep{weber_applying_2021}, following the worked examples at \url{https://opensource.nibr.com/RBesT/articles/introduction_normal.html}. 
Particularly, we fit the hierarchical model via MCMC by assuming a weakly informative Normal on $\mu$ centred at zero with scale $\sigma$, and a
HalfNormal on $\tau$ with scale $\sigma/2$, encoding the prior belief that between-trial heterogeneity is unlikely to exceed half
the measurement scale. Then, the package exploits EM algorithm to select the number of components $K$ automatically by AIC that can then be robustified.

Applying these steps to the six historical placebo arms from the running example yields the hierarchical model fit
shown in Figure~\ref{fig:map_forest}. Figure~\ref{fig:map_robust_plot} then compares the 1-component Normal approximation (used in
the running example for its closed-form tractability) to the 3 component mixture selected by AIC and its robustified version.

<<map_setup, include=FALSE, cache=TRUE, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE>>=
library(RBesT)
library(ggplot2)
crohn_sigma <- 88
dat <- crohn
dat$y.se <- crohn_sigma / sqrt(dat$n)
set.seed(1)
map_mcmc <- gMAP(cbind(y, y.se) ~ 1 | study,
  weights = n, data = dat,
  family = gaussian,
  beta.prior = cbind(0, crohn_sigma),
  tau.dist = "HalfNormal", tau.prior = cbind(0, crohn_sigma / 2)
)
map_fit_1 <- automixfit(map_mcmc, Nc = 1)   # single Normal
map_fit_3 <- automixfit(map_mcmc)            # full mixture: selected by AIC
ess_map_1 <- round(ess(map_fit_1))
ess_map_3 <- round(ess(map_fit_3))
@

<<map_forest, echo=FALSE, cache=TRUE, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE, fig.height=4, fig.width=8, dev='cairo_pdf', fig.cap="Forest plot of the hierarchical meta-analysis fit. Each row shows a historical placebo arm $\\theta_h$ (posterior mean and 95\\% credible interval). The bottom row (\\textit{MAP}) represents the posterior predictive for the new trial's $\\theta_C$.">>=
forest_plot(
  map_mcmc,
  prob       = 0.95,      # 95% credible intervals
  est        = "MAP",    # show both Mean (posterior of mu) and MAP predictive rows
  model      = "stratified",  # show only raw per-study estimates
  point_est  = "median",  # posterior median as point estimate
  size       = 1.25,
  linewidth  = 1.25
) + ylab(expression(theta[C])) #for some reason they made the plot inverted x and y axis 
@

<<map_robust_plot, echo=FALSE, cache=TRUE, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE, fig.height=4, fig.width=8, dev='cairo_pdf', fig.cap=paste0("MAP prior approximations and robust extension. The 1-component Normal (ESS $= ", ess_map_1, "$, used in the running example) and the ", ncol(map_fit_3), "-component mixture selected by AIC (ESS $= ", ess_map_3, "$) both approximate $\\pi_{\\mathrm{MAP}}$. Adding a weakly informative $\\mathcal{N}(-50,\\,88^2)$ component with weight $\\omega = 0.2$ yields the Robust MAP.")>>=
theta_grid <- seq(-300, 180, length.out = 600)
map_robust <- robustify(map_fit_3, weight = 0.2, mean = -50, sigma = crohn_sigma)
labels <- c(
  sprintf("1-component MAP (ESS = %d)", ess_map_1),
  sprintf("%d-component MAP (ESS = %d)", ncol(map_fit_3), ess_map_3),
  sprintf("Robust MAP (omega = 0.2)")
)
df_map <- rbind(
  data.frame(theta = theta_grid, density = dmix(map_fit_1,  theta_grid), prior = labels[1]),
  data.frame(theta = theta_grid, density = dmix(map_fit_3,  theta_grid), prior = labels[2]),
  data.frame(theta = theta_grid, density = dmix(map_robust, theta_grid), prior = labels[3])
)
df_map$prior <- factor(df_map$prior, levels = labels)
ggplot(df_map, aes(x = theta, y = density, colour = prior, linetype = prior)) +
  geom_line(linewidth = 0.9) +
  scale_colour_manual(values = c("#0072B2", "#D55E00", "#009E73")) +
  scale_linetype_manual(values = c("solid", "dashed", "dotdash")) +
  labs(x = expression(theta[C]), y = "Density", colour = NULL, linetype = NULL) +
  coord_cartesian(xlim = c(-150, 70)) +
  theme_minimal(base_size = 12) +
  theme(legend.position = "bottom")
@




\subsection{Metrics to evaluate Bayesian Designs}
Frequentist Type I Error and power are well-established metrics used to evaluate traditional trial designs. However, it is documented that when informative priors are incorporated into a 
design, strict point-wise control of the frequentist Type I Error is impossible \citep{viele_use_2014, kopp-schneider_power_2020}. Consequently, in the Bayesian framework, alternative metrics are often evaluated, such 
as Assurance \citep{ohagan_assurance_2005} or Predictive Power \citep{micheloud_power_2022, spiegelhalter_monitoring_1986}.



All these metrics are special cases of a common metric $m$ \citep{best_beyond_2025}, which can be expressed as the expected probability of study success, integrated over a 
specified prior distribution $\pi_D$ for a parameter $\theta$:
\begin{equation} \label{eq:m_general}
m = \int \Pr\left(\text{Success} \mid \theta\right) \pi_D(\theta) \, d\theta
\end{equation}


It is crucial to differentiate between design and analysis prior \citep{ohagan_bayesian_2001}.  
\begin{itemize}
  \item The analysis prior $\pi_A(\theta)$ is the distribution used during the actual analysis of the trial, to derive the posterior and evaluate the decision rule (see Equation~\eqref{eq:an_prior}). In this manuscript, 
$\pi_A(\theta)$ incorporates historical data via a Meta-Analytic-Predictive (MAP) prior, but other methods can be employed (see \autoref{subsec:info-borrowing})
  \item  The design prior $\pi_D(\theta)$, (see Equation~\eqref{eq:m_general}) is the assumed distribution of the underlying true parameter (data generating process) used exclusively in the planning stage to evaluate the design's 
operating characteristics across various plausible true scenarios.
\end{itemize}


While the approach of using the exact same prior beliefs for both the data generation and the inference ($\pi_D = \pi_A$) 
is considered the most logically consistent and natural by many Bayesian 
methodologists \citep{ohagan_bayesian_2001}, it may be not sufficient to regulatory authorities. In fact, a more comprehensive sensitivity analysis can be required, in order to evaluate the 
design under "prior-data conflict" or "drift" scenarios, where the prior information used for analysis ($\pi_A$) differs from the true data-generating process ($\pi_D$). 

We will focus on two distinct borrowing approaches \citep{best_beyond_2025}: 
using a prior on the control group $\theta_C$ (known in literature as "hybrid-control trials") or using it on the treatment contrast $\delta$.




\subsubsection{Borrowing on control} \label{sec:pointwise}
When historical borrowing is applied exclusively on the control arm, the operating characteristics depend jointly on the true parameters $\theta_C$ and $\theta_T$. The general metric from Equation~\eqref{eq:m_general} expands
 to a double integral over the joint design prior $\pi_D(\theta_C, \theta_T)$:
\begin{equation} \label{eq:m_joint}
m = \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \Pr\left(\text{Success} \mid \theta_C, \theta_T\right) \pi_D(\theta_C, \theta_T) \, d\theta_T \, d\theta_C
\end{equation}


The traditional definitions of both Type I Error and Power impose deterministic constraints 
on the relationship between the treatment and control parameters. Specifically, we can fix the treatment 
contrast at a constant value $\delta$, such that $\theta_T = \theta_C - \delta$. Therefore, the joint design prior is reduced to a marginal prior on the control group, 
$\pi_D(\theta_C)$, collapsing Equation~\ref{eq:m_joint} into a single integral, and by varying the assumed true effect size $\delta$, we can define two metrics for evaluating the trial design:

\paragraph{Average Type I Error (avgT1E)}
When the true treatment effect is null ($\delta = 0$), we obtain the Average Type I Error \citep{best_beyond_2025}:  
\begin{equation}
  \label{eq:avg-type1-error}
  \text{avgT1E} = \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \Pr\left(\text{Success} \mid \theta_C, \theta_T = \theta_C\right) \pi_D(\theta_C) \, d\theta_C
\end{equation}

\textit{Remark:} by defining $\pi_D(\theta_C)$ as a Dirac delta function centered at a specific value $\theta_C^*$, Equation~\ref{eq:avg-type1-error} collapses 
to the classic frequentist Type I Error at $\theta_C^*$.  It is easy to notice that, in this case,
the conditional T1E is not a single constant, but varies pointwise as a function of $\theta_C^*$. 


\paragraph{Average Power ($\text{avgPower}$)}
When the true treatment effect is assumed to be a specific non-zero value ($\delta \neq 0$),  we obtain the Average Power (at $\delta$):  
\begin{equation} \label{eq:avg-power}
  \text{avgPower}(\delta) = \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \Pr\left(\text{Success} \mid \theta_C, \theta_T = \theta_C - \delta\right) \pi_D(\theta_C) \, d\theta_C
\end{equation}


\textit{Remark:} We use the term "Average Power" rather than "Assurance" for Equation~\ref{eq:avg-power} to avoid confusion. Assurance often 
implies marginalization over a prior distribution for the effect size $\delta$ \citep{ohagan_assurance_2005}. Because our framework fixes 
$\delta$ and integrates solely over the control parameter space $\pi_D(\theta_C)$, "Average Power" prevents ambiguity.

Finally, consistent with the Type I Error case, specifying the design prior $\pi_D(\theta_C)$ as a Dirac delta measure in 
Equation~\eqref{eq:avg-power} recovers the classical frequentist power. In this case, the conditional power becomes a function of both the assumed true control mean 
$\theta_C^*$ and the treatment effect $\delta$.





%% As an alternative, it is also proposed to compute the maximum (or minimum) operating characteristic over a plausible subset of the parameter space, 
%% denoted as $\Theta_C$. Then this restricted maximum Type I Error is expressed as:
%% $$\max_{\theta_C \in \Theta_C} \Pr\left(\text{Success} \mid \theta_C, \theta_T = \theta_C - \delta\right)$$

\paragraph{Analytical results under Normal Priors}

Assuming Normal distributions for both the historical analysis prior ($\pi_A$) and the design prior ($\pi_D$), it is possible to derive closed-form solutions for Equation~\eqref{eq:avg-power} and ~\eqref{eq:avg-type1-error}. 
Complete proofs are reported in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_normal_priors}. 

Let the observed sample means for the control and treatment arms be normally distributed as $\bar{y}_C \mid \theta_C \sim \mathcal{N}(\theta_C, v_C)$ and $\bar{y}_T \mid \theta_T \sim \mathcal{N}(\theta_T, v_T)$, respectively, with $v_{\bullet} = \sigma_{\bullet}^{2}/n_{\bullet}$. 
We incorporate an informative historical analysis prior for the control arm, $\pi_A(\theta_C) \sim \mathcal{N}(\mu_{A,C}, \sigma_{A,C}^2)$, where $\mu_{A,C}$ represents the historical mean of control group and $\sigma_{A,C}^2$ quantifies the prior uncertainty. We assume a non-informative analysis prior for the treatment arm.
The Bayesian Trial is declared a success if $\Pr(\delta > q \mid \bar{y}_C, \bar{y}_T) > p$, consistent with Equation~\eqref{eq:an_prior}.

Let $\hat{\delta}$ denote the posterior mean of the treatment contrast ($\delta$). By Normal-Normal conjugacy, the posterior mean of the control arm is a convex combination governed by $W = \sigma_{A,C}^2 / (\sigma_{A,C}^2 + v_C)$. Consequently, we can rewrite the condition of Success in the form: 

\begin{equation} \label{eq:threshold_k}
    \hat{\delta} > k \qquad \text{with } k = q + z_p \sqrt{V_{\delta}}
\end{equation}


where $q$ is the target efficacy threshold, $z_p$ is the Normal quantile corresponding to the required posterior probability, and $V_{\delta} = \sigma_{A,C}^2 v_C / (\sigma_{A,C}^2 + v_C) + v_T$ is the posterior variance of $\delta$.

\paragraph{Conditional metrics}

By treating the true parameters as fixed constants ($\theta_C = \theta_C^*$ and $\theta_T = \theta_C^* - \delta$) and evaluating the frequentist sampling distribution of the estimator across repeated trials, we obtain the closed-form solutions for the conditional metrics. 

The Conditional Power, evaluated at a specific true control rate $\theta_C^*$ and true effect size $\delta$, is given by:
\begin{equation} \label{eq:power_closed_form}
    \text{Power}(\theta_C^*, \delta) = 1 - \Phi\left( \frac{q + z_p \sqrt{V_{\delta}} - \big[(1 - W)(\mu_{A,C} - \theta_C^*) + \delta\big]}{\sqrt{W^2 v_C + v_T}} \right)
\end{equation}

The conditional Type I Error is simply the special case evaluated under the strict null hypothesis ($\delta = 0$):
\begin{equation} \label{eq:t1e_closed_form}
    \text{T1E}(\theta_C^*) = 1 - \Phi\left( \frac{q + z_p \sqrt{V_{\delta}} - (1 - W)(\mu_{A,C} - \theta_C^*)}{\sqrt{W^2 v_C + v_T}} \right)
\end{equation}


%% When the true reality perfectly aligns with the historical expectation ($\theta_C^* \approx \mu_{A,C}$), the bias term vanishes. The Bayesian threshold ($z_p \sqrt{V_{\delta}}$) is reduced, yielding an increase in Power.
%% If the control arm performs worse than historically expected ($\theta_C^* > \mu_{A,C}$), the prior pulls the estimate down, creating a negative bias that decreases statistical power. Conversely, if the control arm performs better than expected ($\theta_C^* < \mu_{A,C}$), the positive bias inflates power, but at the cost Type I Error inflation.
Note that as $W \to 1$ (a non-informative analysis prior, or an infinitely large control group), the bias term vanishes and the formulation collapses to the classical two-sample $Z$-test power \citep{lachin1981introduction}:
\begin{equation} \label{eq:ztest_power}
    \text{Power}_Z(\delta) = 1 - \Phi\!\left( z_p - \frac{\delta - q}{\sigma\sqrt{\dfrac{1}{n_C} + \dfrac{1}{n_T}}} \right)
\end{equation}

\paragraph{Averaged Metrics}
 When using a design prior $\pi_D(\theta_C) \sim \mathcal{N}(\mu_{D,C}, \sigma_{D,C}^2)$, we apply the Laws of Total Expectation and Total Variance to marginalize out the unknown parameter $\theta_C$. 

The closed-form solution for the Average Power is:
\begin{equation} \label{eq:avgpower_closed_form}
    \text{avgPower}(\delta) = 1 - \Phi\left( \frac{q + z_p \sqrt{V_{\delta}} - \big[(1 - W)(\mu_{A,C} - \mu_{D,C}) + \delta\big]}{\sqrt{W^2 v_C + v_T + (1 - W)^2 \sigma_{D,C}^2}} \right)
\end{equation}

Evaluating this distribution under the null hypothesis ($\delta = 0$) yields the Average Type I Error:
\begin{equation} \label{eq:avgt1e_closed_form}
    \text{avgT1E} = 1 - \Phi\left( \frac{q + z_p \sqrt{V_{\delta}} - (1 - W)(\mu_{A,C} - \mu_{D,C})}{\sqrt{W^2 v_C + v_T + (1 - W)^2 \sigma_{D,C}^2}} \right)
\end{equation}

\paragraph{Observations on Averaged Metrics:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item  The denominator of the averaged metrics includes the term $(1 - W)^2 \sigma_{D,C}^2$. This accounts for the additional uncertainty of integrating over $\pi_D$. Because dividing by a larger variance
     shrinks a $Z$-score toward zero, the larger marginal variance forces average metrics closer to $50\%$ compared to conditional ones (evaluated at $\mu_{D,C}$).
    \item  When the analysis prior and design prior coincide ($\mu_{A,C} = \mu_{D,C}$ and $\sigma_{A,C}^2 = \sigma_{D,C}^2$) \emph{and the null threshold is $q = 0$}, the avgT1E is controlled at exactly $1-p$, aligning with the literature \citep{best_beyond_2025}. 
    \item When $W \to 1$, the formulation collapses to the frequentist two sample Z test.
    \item \citet{best_beyond_2025} evaluates the case of using a completely vague design prior. This leads the marginal variance in the denominator to diverge to infinity. Consequently, both the Average Power
    and the Average Type I Error converge to $\Phi(0) = 0.5$, no matter the choice of the Analysis Prior.
\end{itemize}

When the analysis and design priors coincide ($\mu_{A,C} = \mu_{D,C}$, $\sigma_{A,C} = \sigma_{D,C}$), the bias term vanishes and, as shown in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_normal_priors}, the marginal variance equals $V_\delta$. The Average Power then simplifies to:
\begin{align*}
     \text{avgPower}(\delta) &= 1 - \Phi\left( \frac{z_p \sqrt{V_{\delta}} - \delta}{\sqrt{V_{\delta}}} \right) \\
     &= 1 - \Phi\left( z_p - \frac{\delta}{\sqrt{V_{\delta}}} \right)
\end{align*}


In the figures below, the analysis prior is held fixed ($\mu_{A,C} = -50$, $\sigma_{A,C}^2 = 88^2/20$) while the design prior parameters are varied to represent different assumptions about the true data-generating process.

Figure~\ref{fig:plot_t1e} visualizes how Type I Error reacts to two types of conflict between analysis and design priors: location shift ($\mu_{A,C} \neq \mu_{D,C}$) and scale shift ($\sigma_{A,C} \neq \sigma_{D,C}$). The left panel shows that if the true placebo response is better than expected ($\mu_{D,C} < -50$), the Type I Error increases. The conditional T1E (conditioned on $\theta_C^* = \mu_{D,C}$) is always below the avgT1E. In the right panel, when design uncertainty $\sigma_{D,C}$ is zero we obtain the conditional T1E, while as $\sigma_{D,C} \to \infty$ the Average Type I Error converges to $0.5$, consistent with Equation~\eqref{eq:avgt1e_closed_form}

<<plot_t1e, echo=FALSE, warning=FALSE, message=FALSE, fig.height=4, fig.width=10, dev='cairo_pdf', fig.cap="Left panel shows Type I Error under Location Shift (varying $\\mu_{D,C}$), assuming $\\sigma_{D,C} = \\sigma_{A,C}$. Conditional metric is evaluated at $\\theta_C^* = \\mu_{D,C}$. Right panel shows Average Type I Error under Scale Shift (varying $\\sigma_{D,C}$) while setting $\\mu_{D,C} = \\mu_{A,C}$. The vertical gray dotted lines indicate the points of prior alignment ($\\pi_D = \\pi_A$). The red dashed line represents the nominal value of $0.05$">>=
library(ggplot2)
library(dplyr)
library(tidyr)
library(gridExtra)
library(scales)

# --- Trial Parameters & Functions  ---
sigma <- 88; n_C <- 20; n_T <- 40; n_A <- 20; N_total <- n_C + n_T
v_C <- sigma^2 / n_C; v_T <- sigma^2 / n_T; sigma_A2 <- sigma^2 / n_A; mu_A <- -50
q <- 0; z_p <- qnorm(0.95)
W <- sigma_A2 / (sigma_A2 + v_C)
V_delta <- W * v_C + v_T
V_samp <- W^2 * v_C + v_T

condPower <- function(theta_C_star, delta) {
  num <- q + z_p * sqrt(V_delta) - ((1 - W) * (mu_A - theta_C_star) + delta)
  1 - pnorm(num / sqrt(V_samp))
}
avgPower <- function(mu_D, sigma_D2, delta) {
  num <- q + z_p * sqrt(V_delta) - ((1 - W) * (mu_A - mu_D) + delta)
  1 - pnorm(num / sqrt(V_samp + (1 - W)^2 * sigma_D2))
}
calc_EUII <- function(power, t1e, N) {
  eps <- 1e-10
  p <- pmax(pmin(power, 1 - eps), eps); t <- pmax(pmin(t1e, 1 - eps), eps)

  log_dor <- qlogis(p) - qlogis(t)
  return(exp(log_dor / N))
}

my_theme <- theme_minimal() + theme(legend.position = "bottom", legend.title = element_blank())
my_x_scale <- scale_x_continuous(trans = "log1p", breaks = c(0, 10, 100, 1000, 10000), labels = c("0\n(Cond.)", "10", "100", "1K", "10K"))

# --- T1E Data & Plots ---
df_t1e_loc <- data.frame(mu_D = seq(-90, -10, length.out = 100)) %>%
  mutate(
    `Conditional T1E` = condPower(mu_D, 0),
    `Average T1E` = avgPower(mu_D, sigma_A2, 0)
  ) %>%
  pivot_longer(cols = c(`Conditional T1E`, `Average T1E`), names_to = "Metric", values_to = "Value")

sigmas <- c(0, 10^seq(0, 5, length.out = 99))
df_t1e_scale <- data.frame(sigma_D = sigmas) %>%
  mutate(Avg_T1E = avgPower(mu_A, sigma_D^2, 0))

p1a <- ggplot(df_t1e_loc, aes(x = mu_D, y = Value, color = Metric, linetype = Metric)) +
  geom_line(size = 1.2) +
  geom_hline(yintercept = 0.05, linetype = "dashed", color = "red", linewidth = 0.4) +
  geom_vline(xintercept = mu_A, linetype = "dotted", color = "gray", linewidth = 0.8) +
  scale_color_manual(values = c("Average T1E" = "#E69F00", "Conditional T1E" = "#56B4E9")) +
  labs(x = expression("True Control Mean (" * mu["D,C"] * ")"),
       y = "Type I Error") +
  my_theme

p1b <- ggplot(df_t1e_scale, aes(x = sigma_D, y = Avg_T1E)) +
  geom_line(color = "#E69F00", size = 1.2) +
  geom_hline(yintercept = 0.5, linetype = "dashed", color = "black", linewidth = 0.6) +
  geom_hline(yintercept = 0.05, linetype = "dashed", color = "red", linewidth = 0.4) +
  geom_vline(xintercept = sqrt(sigma_A2), linetype = "dotted", color = "gray", linewidth = 0.8) +
  my_x_scale +
  coord_cartesian(ylim = c(0, 0.6)) +
  labs(x = expression(sigma["D,C"]),
       y = "avgT1E") +
  my_theme

grid.arrange(p1a, p1b, ncol = 2)
@

Figure~\ref{fig:plot_power} shows the behavior of statistical power assuming $\mu_{D,C} = \mu_{A,C} = -50$. In the left panel $\sigma_{D,C} = \sigma_{A,C}$ and we vary $\delta$. As already observed, Average Power is always closer to $50\%$ than Conditional Power. The right panel shows how avgPower at $\delta = 60$ changes as design uncertainty $\sigma_{D,C}$ increases: when $\sigma_{D,C} = 0$ we obtain the conditional power, while as $\sigma_{D,C} \to \infty$, avgPower reaches $50\%$.

<<plot_power, echo=FALSE, warning=FALSE, message=FALSE, dev='cairo_pdf', fig.height=4, fig.width=10, fig.cap="Left panel shows Conditional and Average Statistical Power evaluated across true treatment effects ($\\delta$), assuming perfectly aligned priors ($\\mu_{A,C} = \\mu_{D,C}$ and $\\sigma_{A,C} = \\sigma_{D,C}$). Right panel shows Average Power evaluated at $\\delta = 60$ as design uncertainty ($\\sigma_{D,C}$) increases, holding $\\mu_{D,C} = \\mu_{A,C}$. The vertical gray dotted lines indicate the points of prior alignment ($\\pi_D = \\pi_A$).">>=
# --- Power Data & Plots ---
df_pow_loc <- data.frame(delta = seq(0, 100, length.out = 100)) %>%
  mutate(
    `Conditional Power` = condPower(mu_A, delta),
    `Average Power` = avgPower(mu_A, sigma_A2, delta)
  ) %>%
  pivot_longer(cols = c(`Conditional Power`, `Average Power`), names_to = "Metric", values_to = "Value")

df_pow_scale <- data.frame(sigma_D = sigmas) %>%
  mutate(Avg_Power = avgPower(mu_A, sigma_D^2, 60))

p2a <- ggplot(df_pow_loc, aes(x = delta, y = Value, color = Metric, linetype = Metric)) +
  geom_line(size = 1) +
  scale_color_manual(values = c("Average Power" = "#E69F00", "Conditional Power" = "#56B4E9")) +
  labs(x = expression("True Effect (" * delta * ")"),
       y = "Power") +
  my_theme

p2b <- ggplot(df_pow_scale, aes(x = sigma_D, y = Avg_Power)) +
  geom_line(color = "#E69F00", size = 1.2) +
  geom_hline(yintercept = 0.5, linetype = "dashed", color="black", linewidth = 0.6) +
  geom_vline(xintercept = sqrt(sigma_A2), linetype = "dotted", color = "gray", linewidth = 0.8) +
  my_x_scale +
  coord_cartesian(ylim = c(0.4, 1.0)) +
  labs(x = expression(sigma["D,C"]),
       y = "avgPower") +
  my_theme

grid.arrange(p2a, p2b, ncol = 2)
@

\paragraph{Sample size computation}

For sample size computation, certain assumptions allow us to reparametrize Equation~\eqref{eq:avgpower_closed_form} into a compact form. 
First, it is reasonable to perform sample size computations using a correctly specified Analysis prior ($\mu_{A,C} = \mu_{D,C}$ and $\sigma_{A,C} = \sigma_{D,C}$). Then we can assume a common patient variance $\sigma^2$ across both arms, such that $v_C = \sigma^2/n_C$ and $v_T = \sigma^2/n_T$.
We can also define the effective sample size (ESS) for the Analysis prior $n_A$ (note that $n_A = n_D$), and write $\sigma_{A,C}^2 = \sigma^2/n_A$.

Under these assumptions, the weight becomes $W = n_C/(n_C+n_A)$ and the bias term $(1-W)(\mu_{A,C}-\mu_{D,C})$ vanishes entirely. We further define two design ratios:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $r_A = n_A/n_C$: the historical-to-control ESS ratio (implying $W = 1/(1+r_A)$).
    \item $r_T = n_T/n_C$: the allocation ratio.
\end{itemize}


Substituting the ESS parameterization into Equation~\eqref{eq:avgpower_closed_form} and simplifying yields:
\begin{equation} \label{eq:avgpower_ess}
    \text{avgPower}(\delta) = 1 - \Phi \left( z_p - \frac{\delta - q}{\sigma \sqrt{\dfrac{1}{n_C + n_A} + \dfrac{1}{n_T}}} \right)
\end{equation}
  
\textit{Remark}
Equation~\eqref{eq:avgpower_ess} is identical to Equation~\eqref{eq:ztest_power} evaluated with $n_C^* = n_C + n_A$ effective control patients.
This gives the ESS of the analysis prior a precise meaning: under a correctly specified prior, the $n_A$ historical pseudo-observations are worth exactly $n_A$ additional control patients in terms of average power.
This equivalence holds only under a correctly specified prior; misspecification reintroduces the bias term and can invalidate avgT1E and avgPower results.

Setting Equation~\eqref{eq:avgpower_ess} equal to the target power $1-\beta$ and fixing the allocation ratio $r_T$ (so $n_T = r_T n_C$) yields:
$$ \frac{1}{n_C + n_A} + \frac{1}{r_T\, n_C} = \frac{(\delta - q)^2}{\sigma^2(z_p + z_{1-\beta})^2} =: \frac{1}{K} $$
This results in a quadratic equation in terms of $n_C$:
$$ r_T\, n_C^2 + \bigl[r_T n_A - K(r_T + 1)\bigr]\, n_C - K n_A = 0 $$
The positive root provides the closed-form solution for the required control sample size:
\begin{equation} \label{eq:n_c_formula}
    n_C = \frac{K(r_T+1) - r_T n_A + \sqrt{\bigl[K(r_T+1) - r_T n_A\bigr]^2 + 4\, r_T\, K\, n_A}}{2\, r_T}, \quad K = \frac{\sigma^2(z_p + z_{1 -\beta})^2}{(\delta - q)^2}
\end{equation}

As $n_A \to 0$ (the scenario with no historical borrowing), Equation~\eqref{eq:n_c_formula} converges to $n_C \to K(1 + 1/r_T)$, which is exactly the classical frequentist two-sample $Z$-test formula with unequal group sizes. 


Thus, Average power appears to be a more natural metric for sample size planning in this Bayesian framework.
Infact, solving $\text{condPower}(\mu_{A,C},\delta) = 1-\beta$ for $n_C$ requires $z_p\sqrt{V_\delta} + z_{1-\beta}\sqrt{V_\text{samp}} = \delta - q$, and since $V_\delta$ and $V_\text{samp}$ depend both on $n_C$, no closed-form sample size formula exists and numerical inversion is needed.
Average power avoids this: under the alignment assumptions $V_\text{marg} = V_\delta$, the formula reduces to a single square-root term, and the inversion yields the closed-form quadratic in Equation~\eqref{eq:n_c_formula}.
This simplification also gives $n_A$ an additive role: in Equation~\eqref{eq:avgpower_ess}, $n_A$ enters only through $n_C + n_A$, so each historical pseudo-observation reduces the required control sample size by exactly one.
By contrast, in the conditional power formula $n_A$ appears both through $V_\delta$ and $V_\text{samp}$, which depend on $n_A$ in different ways, so no such additive interpretation holds.

\textit{Running example.} Using the parameters from the running example ($\sigma = 88$, $n_A = n_D = 20$, $r_T = 2$, $\delta = 60$, $q = 0$), we obtain:
$$ K = \frac{88^2 \times (1.645 + 0.842)^2}{60^2} \approx 13.31 $$
Substituting into Equation~\eqref{eq:n_c_formula} gives $n_C \approx 11.5 \to 12$, so $n_C = 12$ control patients ($n_T = 24$, $n_{\text{trial}} = 36$) achieves just above $80\%$ average power. The actual design ($n_C = 20$) yields $\text{avgPower}(60) \approx 92\%$, consistent with Figure~\ref{fig:plot_power} and with Equation~\eqref{eq:ztest_power} evaluated at $n_C^* = 40$.

Figure~\ref{fig:plot_avgpower_nc} shows how average power varies with $n_C$ for several values of $n_A$: borrowing more historical data shifts the power curve left, reducing the required $n_C$. The $n_A = 0$ curve coincides with the classical $Z$-test power (Equation~\eqref{eq:ztest_power}) while the $n_A = 20$ curve corresponds to the running example.

<<plot_avgpower_nc, echo=FALSE, warning=FALSE, message=FALSE, fig.height=3.5, fig.width=6, dev='cairo_pdf', fig.cap="Average power vs.\ control sample size $n_C$ ($\\delta = 60$, $\\sigma = 88$, $r_T = 2$, $q = 0$) for varying ESS $n_A$. The horizontal dashed line represents 80\\% power target; while the two dotted verticals the minimum required $n_C$ (Eq.~\\ref{eq:n_c_formula} with $n_A=20$) and actual design value $n_C = 20$.">>=
avgPower_ess <- function(delta, n_C, n_A, r_T, sigma, z_p, q = 0) {
  n_T <- r_T * n_C
  1 - pnorm(z_p - (delta - q) / (sigma * sqrt(1 / (n_C + n_A) + 1 / n_T)))
}

n_C_seq <- seq(4, 40, by = 0.5)
n_A_vals <- c(0, 10, 20, 30)
df_ss <- do.call(rbind, lapply(n_A_vals, function(na) {
  data.frame(
    n_C   = n_C_seq,
    power = avgPower_ess(delta = 60, n_C = n_C_seq, n_A = na,
                         r_T = 2, sigma = 88, z_p = qnorm(0.95)),
    n_A   = factor(paste0("n_A = ", na))
  )
}))

K_req   <- 88^2 * (qnorm(0.95) + qnorm(0.80))^2 / 60^2
b_req   <- K_req * (2 + 1) - 2 * 20
n_C_req <- (b_req + sqrt(b_req^2 + 4 * 2 * K_req * 20)) / (2 * 2)  # Bayesian (n_A=20)

legend_labels <- c("n_A = 0 (Z-test)", "n_A = 10", "n_A = 20", "n_A = 30")

ggplot(df_ss, aes(x = n_C, y = power, colour = n_A, linetype = n_A)) +
  geom_line(linewidth = 0.8) +
  geom_hline(yintercept = 0.8, linetype = "dashed", colour = "grey50") +
  geom_vline(xintercept = n_C_req, linetype = "dotted", colour = "grey40") +
  geom_vline(xintercept = 20,      linetype = "dotted", colour = "grey40") +
  annotate("text", x = n_C_req - 0.6, y = 0.15, label = paste0("n_C=", ceiling(n_C_req)),
           hjust = 1, size = 3, colour = "grey30") +
  annotate("text", x = 20 + 0.6, y = 0.15, label = "n_C=20",
           hjust = 0, size = 3, colour = "grey30") +
  scale_y_continuous(labels = scales::percent_format(accuracy = 1), limits = c(0, 1)) +
  scale_colour_brewer(palette = "Set1", labels = legend_labels) +
  scale_linetype_discrete(labels = legend_labels) +
  labs(x = expression(n[C]), y = "Average Power",
       colour = NULL, linetype = NULL) +
  my_theme +
  theme(legend.position = "right")
@


\paragraph{Computational Framework \note{TO DO: decide wheter to keep or not: it is just how the metric is computed in R}}
We will now define the computational framework for the average metric, obtained extending the package \citep{weber2021applying}

Given $y_T$ and $y_C$ the sample means for the treatment and control arms, respectively. 

Success is determined by a decision function $D(y_T, y_C) \in \{0, 1\}$, which evaluates whether the posterior distribution of the treatment contrast, 
  $\delta = \theta_C - \theta_T$, satisfies a set of predefined conditions. For instance, a standard design requires the posterior probability to 
  exceed a specific threshold: $\Pr(\delta > q \mid y_T, y_C) > p$ for threshold $q$ and probability $p$.

When evaluating multiple conditions simultaneously, such as in dual-criterion designs requiring both statistical significance and clinical relevance 
\citep{roychoudhury_beyond_2018}, the decision is formulated as the product of Heaviside step functions for each condition\footnote{Note that this product 
formulation naturally generalizes to an arbitrary number of conditions} $H(x)$, where $H(x) = 1$ for $x > 0$ and $0$ for $x \le 0$:

$$D(y_T, y_C) = \prod_{i} H\Big(\Pr(\delta \ge q_{c,i} \mid y_T, y_C) - p_{c,i}\Big)$$ 



The specification of priors, the sample sizes ($n_T$, $n_C$), and the decision function $D(y_T, y_C)$ uniquely define a conditional Decision Boundary 
$D_T(y_C)$ for each observed control outcome $y_C$,
$$D_T(y_C) = \max\{y_T : D(y_T, y_C) = 1\}$$ 
This represents the maximal threshold the treatment data $y_T$ must satisfy to declare success.

Given this boundary, we can compute the conditional probability of success $\Pr(\text{Success} \mid \theta_T, \theta_C)$ required in Equation \ref{eq:m_joint}. Assuming
 true parameters $\theta_T$ and $\theta_C$, the overall probability of a success is the expectation of crossing the boundary $D_T(y_C)$ over the sampling 
 distribution of the control data:
\begin{equation}
  \label{eq:oc2S_general}
  \Pr(\text{Success} \mid \theta_T, \theta_C) = \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} F_T\left(D_T(y_C) \mid \theta_T\right) f_C\left(y_C \mid \theta_C\right) \, dy_C
\end{equation}
where $F_T\left(\cdot \mid \theta_T\right)$ is the cumulative distribution function of the treatment statistic evaluated at the decision boundary, and
 $f_C\left(\cdot \mid \theta_C\right)$ is the probability density function of the control statistic.

Because we are working with normally distributed endpoints, the sampling distributions for the sample means are normal: 
$y_m \sim \mathcal{N}(\theta_m, SEM_m^2)$ for $m \in \{T, C\}$, where $SEM_m = \sigma_m / \sqrt{n_m}$. Consequently, the general integral in 
Equation \ref{eq:oc2S_general} can be evaluated directly using the standard normal CDF ($\Phi$) and PDF ($\phi$):
\begin{equation}
  \label{eq:oc2S_normal}
  \Pr(\text{Success} \mid \theta_T, \theta_C) = \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \Phi\left(\frac{D_T(y_C) - \theta_T}{SEM_T}\right) \frac{1}{SEM_C} \phi\left(\frac{y_C - \theta_C}{SEM_C}\right) \, dy_C
\end{equation}
 

