\subsection{Application 1: Borrowing on the Control Arm}
\label{sec:application1}

We evaluate the design of a Proof-of-Concept (PoC) study in Crohn’s disease, an inflammatory bowel disease. The primary endpoint is the change from baseline to week 6 in the Crohn’s Disease Activity Index (CDAI), a 
continuous measure where lower scores indicate clinical improvement.

The original trial evaluated the efficacy of secukinumab against a placebo \citep{hueber2012secukinumab}. 
Let $\theta_C$ and $\theta_T$ the true underlying mean changes for the control and treatment arms, respectively. 
The treatment effect is defined as the contrast $\delta = \theta_C - \theta_T$, where $\delta > 0$ represents a treatment benefit.


To improve trial efficiency, the original design derived an informative analysis prior ($\pi_A$) for the placebo arm using 671 
historical placebo patients from six previous Crohn’s disease trials via a Meta-Analytic-Predictive (MAP) approach.



\paragraph{Decision Rules}
At any given analysis, a successful terminal decision requires both strong evidence of a positive treatment effect and sufficient evidence of a clinically relevant 
effect size:
$$
\Pr(\delta > 0 \mid \mathcal{D}, \pi_A) \ge 0.95 \quad \text{and} \quad \Pr(\delta > 50 \mid \mathcal{D}, \pi_A) \ge 0.50
$$

where $\mathcal{D}$ denotes the observed data up to that point. Furthermore, sequential designs in this application incorporate a Bayesian futility criterion. 
The trial can be stopped for futility if it is highly probable that the treatment effect is less than 40:

$$
\Pr(\delta < 40 \mid \mathcal{D}, \pi_A) \ge 0.90
 $$



\paragraph{Design Variations and Evaluation Strategy}
The literature presents varying implementations of this trial. The original protocol \citep{hueber2012secukinumab} planned two interim analyses, occurring when 17 and 41 patients, 
respectively, reached the 6 week evaluation time point. Conversely, \citet{gsponer_practical_2014} modified this into a two-stage group sequential design with a 1:2 allocation 
ratio (10 placebo and 20 experimental patients per stage). Taking a simpler approach, \citet{best_beyond_2025} evaluated the trial as a fixed design using 20 and 40 
individuals for the control and treatment arms, omitting the dual success criterion. Another cause of divergence is terminal decision: while \citet{gsponer_practical_2014} 
allowed a final "indeterminate" result by evaluating futility at the final analysis, we follow the original formulation, which restricts the final stage to a 
binary outcome (success or failure) by removing the terminal futility check.



Therefore, we will compute $\text{avgT1E}$, $\text{avgPower}$, and $\text{EUII}$ across three design variations of this trial:
 \begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Fixed Design:} A single-stage design (analogous to the evaluation by \citet{best_beyond_2025})  
  \item \textbf{One Interim Analysis:} An evaluation of the two-stage group-sequential design proposed by \citet{gsponer_practical_2014}.
  \item \textbf{Two Interim Analyses:} An evaluation of the original three-stage trial protocol \citep{hueber2012secukinumab}.

\end{enumerate}


Because the closed-form solutions derived in Section \ref{sec:pointwise} apply only to strictly Normal priors, we structure our evaluation in two phases. 
First, we compute the analytical operating characteristics assuming a strictly Normal analysis and design prior. Then, we transition 
to numerical evaluation, using a grid of analysis priors ($\pi_A$) and design priors ($\pi_D$) that are not strictly Normal, inspecting a sequence of true treatment 
effects $\delta$.




\paragraph{Analysis Priors ($\pi_A$)}
The analysis prior $\pi_A(\theta_C)$ determines the degree of historical information incorporated into the final posterior decision:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Normal MAP:} A single-component Normal prior obtained using the MAP approach on historical data to evaluate closed form results: $\pi_{\text{Normal}}(\theta_C) \sim \mathcal{N}(-50,\, 88^2/20)$: 
  \item \textbf{3-Component MAP:} A mixture of three Normals obtained using the MAP approach: 
  $\pi_{\text{MAP}}(\theta_C) = 0.51\,\mathcal{N}(-51,\,19.9^2) + 0.44\,\mathcal{N}(-46.8,\,7.6^2) + 0.05\,\mathcal{N}(-54.1,\,51.7^2)$
  \item \textbf{Robust MAP ($\omega \in \{0.2, 0.4, 0.6, 0.8\}$):} Four robust mixture priors constructed by assigning a weight $\omega$ to a non-informative component and $(1-\omega)$ to the MAP 3-component prior. 
  The mean of the vague component is set to $-50$ to be consistent with the historical data, and its standard deviation is set to $\sigma = 88$, to be approximately equivalent to one subject's 
  worth of information. The value of $\omega$ reflects our prior belief about the possibility of non-exchangeability between the placebo effect from the historical studies and the 
  placebo effect in the new study.
  \item \textbf{Vague:} A non-informative prior representing a design with no historical borrowing, serving as the frequentist baseline: $\pi_{\text{vague}}(\theta_C) = \mathcal{N}(-50,\,8800^2)$
\end{enumerate}

 
Importantly, we follow the traditional approach of using a unit-information prior (UIP) for the robust component, although alternative solutions have recently been proposed (see Section~\ref{subsec:formulating_priors})

\paragraph{Design Priors ($\pi_D$)}
The design prior $\pi_D(\theta_C)$ represents the assumed true data-generating process for the control arm ($\theta_C$):
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Dirac:} A Dirac measure at $\theta_C^* = -50$, it recovers the classical conditional Type I Error and Power.
  \item \textbf{3-Component MAP:} A design prior exactly matching the corresponding 3-Component MAP analysis prior.
  \item \textbf{Skeptical:} Following the terminology of \citet{best_beyond_2025}, this prior corresponds to the posterior distribution of a stand-alone analysis of the historical study with the “most extreme” placebo effect: $\pi_{\text{skep}}(\theta_C) = \mathcal{N}(-90,\,25^2)$. Because the placebo is exceptionally effective, this scenario is highly "skeptical" of the experimental treatment's ability to demonstrate superiority.
  \item \textbf{Misspecified:} A hypothetical prior-data conflict scenario where the true control arm performs much worse than historically predicted: $\pi_{\text{miss}}(\theta_C) = \mathcal{N}(-10,\,25^2)$.
\end{enumerate}




\subsubsection{Fixed Design}
\label{sec:fixed_design}
Following \citep{best_beyond_2025}, we fix the sample size at $n_C = 20$ and $n_T = 40$ and analyze the single posterior decision rule $\Pr(\delta > 0 \mid \mathcal{D}, \pi_A) \ge 0.95$.\footnote{\citet{best_beyond_2025} originally used a threshold of $0.975$; we adopt $0.95$ throughout for consistency with the first component of the dual criterion introduced in Section~\ref{sec:dual_criterion}.}

\paragraph{Normal priors case}
The closed-form results under Normal priors were developed as a running example throughout Section~\ref{sec:pointwise} and Section~\ref{subsec:euii}; see Figures~\ref{fig:plot_t1e}--\ref{fig:plot_euii}.
While the strictly Normal formulation yields tractable closed-form solutions, it can be fragile under prior misspecification.
To achieve robustness required for actual clinical application, we now turn to robust mixture priors.

\paragraph{Mixture priors}

The Normal-prior closed-form results above serve as a tractable baseline, but MAP priors are usually mixtures of Normals, and robust extensions further add a vague component. Because no closed form exists for these cases, operating characteristics are computed numerically using \texttt{RBesT::oc2S} and a numerical integration routine for the averaged metrics.

\textbf{Conditional Operating Characteristics.}
Figure~\ref{fig:plot_mix_cond} shows the conditional Type I Error ($\delta = 0$, left) and conditional Power at $\delta = 60$ (right) as functions of the fixed true control mean $\theta_C^*$.
The vague prior yields the flat frequentist baseline: Type I Error is exactly $0.05$ everywhere and power is determined solely by the sample size. As historical borrowing increases (decreasing $\omega$), Type I Error inflates with increasing severity when $\theta_C^* \ll -50$ (skeptical scenario) and is increasingly suppressed well below $\alpha$ when $\theta_C^* \gg -50$ (misspecified scenario). The dotted vertical line marks the prior mean $\mu_{A,C} = -50$. Robust MAP priors exhibit a monotone moderation: 
as $\omega$ increases, the curve is pulled toward the flat vague baseline, capping the T1E at the cost of reduced Power. 

<<plot_mix_cond, echo=FALSE, warning=FALSE, message=FALSE, fig.height=4, fig.width=10, dev='cairo_pdf', fig.cap="Conditional metrics for all six analysis priors as a function of the fixed true control mean $\\theta_C^*$. Left: conditional Type I Error ($\\delta = 0$). Right: conditional Power at $\\delta = 60$. The Vague prior (dashed) provides the frequentist reference. The dotted vertical line marks the prior mean $\\mu_{A,C} = -50$.">>=
library(ggplot2)
library(dplyr)
library(gridExtra)

res_cond   <- readRDS("../Output/FIXED_cond.RDS")
df_cond    <- res_cond$df_cond
prior_names <- c("MAP", "Robust (w=0.2)", "Robust (w=0.4)",
                 "Robust (w=0.6)", "Robust (w=0.8)", "Vague")
df_cond$Prior <- factor(df_cond$Prior, levels = prior_names)
mu_A <- -50

my_theme <- theme_minimal() +
  theme(legend.position = "bottom", legend.title = element_blank())

p_cond_T1E <- ggplot(df_cond,
    aes(x = theta_C, y = T1E, color = Prior, linetype = Prior == "Vague")) +
  geom_line(linewidth = 1.2) +
  geom_hline(yintercept = 0.05, linetype = "dashed",
             color = "black", linewidth = 0.6) +
  geom_vline(xintercept = mu_A, linetype = "dotted",
             color = "gray", linewidth = 0.8) +
  scale_linetype_manual(values = c("TRUE" = "dashed", "FALSE" = "solid"),
                        guide = "none") +
  scale_color_viridis_d(option = "turbo", direction = -1) +
  labs(x = expression("True Control Mean (" * theta[C]^"*" * ")"),
       y = "Conditional Type I Error") +
  my_theme

p_cond_Power <- ggplot(df_cond,
    aes(x = theta_C, y = Power, color = Prior, linetype = Prior == "Vague")) +
  geom_line(linewidth = 1.2) +
  geom_vline(xintercept = mu_A, linetype = "dotted",
             color = "gray", linewidth = 0.8) +
  scale_linetype_manual(values = c("TRUE" = "dashed", "FALSE" = "solid"),
                        guide = "none") +
  scale_color_viridis_d(option = "turbo", direction = -1) +
  labs(x = expression("True Control Mean (" * theta[C]^"*" * ")"),
       y = bquote("Conditional Power (" * delta * " = 60)")) +
  my_theme

grid.arrange(p_cond_T1E, p_cond_Power, ncol = 2)
@


\textbf{Average Metrics.}
By marginalizing the conditional curves over a design prior $\pi_D(\theta_C)$, we obtain the Average Type I Error (avgT1E) and Average Power for each of the 24 analysis$\times$design prior combinations.
Figure~\ref{fig:plot_mix_avg} displays on the $x$-axis the design prior (representing different assumptions about the true data-generating process) and each line as a given analysis prior.
When the analysis and design prior are aligned (MAP analysis, MAP design), avgT1E is at the nominal $0.05$, consistent with the theoretical property. Under the skeptical design prior ($\mu_{D,C} = -90$), more informative analysis priors incur into larger avgT1E inflation. The misspecified prior ($\mu_{D,C} = -10$) drives the opposite effect: more informative prior overcorrects the control mean downward, suppressing both avgT1E and Average Power simultaneously, making the trial conservative but underpowered.
Note that the Dirac design prior ($\pi_D = \delta_{\{-50\}}$) concentrates all mass at the prior mean, so the averaged metrics reduce exactly to the conditional counterparts evaluated at $\theta_C^* = -50$; it serves as the best-case reference where the truth is deterministic.


<<plot_mix_avg, echo=FALSE, warning=FALSE, message=FALSE, fig.height=4, fig.width=10, dev='cairo_pdf', fig.cap="Average Type I Error (left) and Average Power at $\\delta = 60$ (right) for all combinations of analysis prior and design prior. Each line connects values for a single analysis prior across the four design prior scenarios. The dashed horizontal line marks the nominal $\\alpha = 0.05$.">>=
library(ggplot2)
library(dplyr)
library(gridExtra)

res_avg      <- readRDS("../Output/FIXED_euii.RDS")
prior_names  <- c("MAP", "Robust (w=0.2)", "Robust (w=0.4)",
                  "Robust (w=0.6)", "Robust (w=0.8)", "Vague")
dprior_names <- c("Dirac (−50)", "MAP", "Skeptical (−90)", "Misspecified (−10)")
df_avg <- res_avg$df_euii |>
  dplyr::filter(delta == 60) |>
  dplyr::mutate(
    Analysis_Prior = factor(Analysis_Prior, levels = prior_names),
    Design_Prior   = factor(Design_Prior,   levels = dprior_names)
  )

my_theme <- theme_minimal() +
  theme(legend.position = "bottom", legend.title = element_blank())

p_avg_T1E <- ggplot(df_avg,
    aes(x = Design_Prior, y = avgT1E,
        color = Analysis_Prior, group = Analysis_Prior,
        linetype = Analysis_Prior == "Vague")) +
  geom_point(size = 2.5) +
  geom_line(linewidth = 1.0) +
  geom_hline(yintercept = 0.05, linetype = "dashed",
             color = "black", linewidth = 0.6) +
  scale_linetype_manual(values = c("TRUE" = "dashed", "FALSE" = "solid"),
                        guide = "none") +
  scale_color_viridis_d(option = "turbo", direction = -1) +
  labs(x = "Design Prior", y = "Average Type I Error") +
  my_theme

p_avg_Power <- ggplot(df_avg,
    aes(x = Design_Prior, y = avgPower,
        color = Analysis_Prior, group = Analysis_Prior,
        linetype = Analysis_Prior == "Vague")) +
  geom_point(size = 2.5) +
  geom_line(linewidth = 1.0) +
  scale_linetype_manual(values = c("TRUE" = "dashed", "FALSE" = "solid"),
                        guide = "none") +
  scale_color_viridis_d(option = "turbo", direction = -1) +
  labs(x = "Design Prior",
       y = bquote("Average Power (" * delta * " = 60)")) +
  my_theme

grid.arrange(p_avg_T1E, p_avg_Power, ncol = 2)
@


\textbf{EUII.}
Figure~\ref{fig:plot_mix_euii} plots the EUII at $\delta = 60$ against the robustness weight $\omega$, with one curve per design prior. The dotted horizontal line marks the vague prior baseline ($\omega = 1$). Reading the four curves together can constitute a sensitivity analysis over the data-generating process: for any proposed $\omega$, the investigator can read off the expected per-patient efficiency under each scenario simultaneously. Under the aligned design prior (MAP), EUII is strictly decreasing in $\omega$ and always above the baseline, since more borrowing is better when the prior is correct. Under the skeptical design prior, all informative analysis priors fall below the vague baseline and $\omega = 1$ (no borrowing) is optimal. 
Under the misspecified design prior, EUII also lies above the baseline for all $\omega$, but this is misleading: the prior suppresses avgT1E so aggressively that the DOR is inflated despite average Power dropping to around $36\%$ at $\delta = 60$. In this context, EUII should always be read alongside average Power. The choice of $\omega$ thus reflects the investigator's belief about which design prior is most plausible: the more confident one is in prior alignment, the smaller the $\omega$ that can be justified; the more one fears prior-data conflict, the larger $\omega$ must be.

<<plot_mix_euii, echo=FALSE, warning=FALSE, message=FALSE, fig.height=4, fig.width=8, dev='cairo_pdf', fig.cap="EUII at $\\delta = 60$ as a function of the robustness weight $\\omega$ for each design prior. The dotted horizontal line marks the vague-prior baseline ($\\omega = 1$). Under aligned priors, EUII is maximized at full borrowing ($\\omega = 0$); under the skeptical design prior it collapses below the baseline for informative analysis priors.">>=
library(ggplot2)
library(dplyr)

res_euii     <- readRDS("../Output/FIXED_euii.RDS")
prior_names  <- c("MAP", "Robust (w=0.2)", "Robust (w=0.4)",
                  "Robust (w=0.6)", "Robust (w=0.8)", "Vague")
dprior_names <- c("Dirac (−50)", "MAP", "Skeptical (−90)", "Misspecified (−10)")
df_euii <- res_euii$df_euii |>
  dplyr::mutate(
    Analysis_Prior = factor(Analysis_Prior, levels = prior_names),
    Design_Prior   = factor(Design_Prior,   levels = dprior_names)
  )

df_euii_50 <- dplyr::filter(df_euii, delta == 60)
euii_vague <- dplyr::filter(df_euii_50, Analysis_Prior == "Vague") |>
  dplyr::pull(EUII) |> unique()

my_theme <- theme_minimal() +
  theme(legend.position = "bottom", legend.title = element_blank())

ggplot(df_euii_50,
       aes(x = omega, y = EUII,
           color = Design_Prior, group = Design_Prior)) +
  geom_line(linewidth = 1.2) +
  geom_point(size = 2.5) +
  geom_hline(yintercept = euii_vague, linetype = "dotted",
             color = "black", linewidth = 0.6) +
  scale_x_continuous(breaks = seq(0, 1, 0.2)) +
  scale_color_viridis_d(option = "turbo", direction = -1) +
  labs(x = expression("Robustness weight (" * omega * ")"),
       y = expression("EUII (" * delta * " = 60)")) +
  my_theme
@

Figure~\ref{fig:plot_mix_euii_delta} shows the EUII as a function of $\delta$ for all 24 analysis-prior/design-prior combinations. Under the aligned design 
prior (MAP), EUII rises monotonically and the ordering is strict: more borrowing (smaller $\omega$) always dominates, confirming that historical information strictly increases EUII when the prior is correctly specified. 
The Dirac design prior, which fixes the truth at the prior mean, acts as the ceiling of efficiency gains.

<<plot_mix_euii_delta, echo=FALSE, warning=FALSE, message=FALSE, fig.height=4, fig.width=12, dev='cairo_pdf', fig.cap="EUII as a function of the treatment effect $\\delta$, faceted by design prior. Color encodes the analysis prior (Vague shown dashed). The dashed horizontal line marks $\\text{EUII} = 1$, the no-information baseline.">>=
library(ggplot2)
library(dplyr)

res_euii     <- readRDS("../Output/FIXED_euii.RDS")
prior_names  <- c("MAP", "Robust (w=0.2)", "Robust (w=0.4)",
                  "Robust (w=0.6)", "Robust (w=0.8)", "Vague")
dprior_names <- c("Dirac (−50)", "MAP", "Skeptical (−90)", "Misspecified (−10)")
df_euii <- res_euii$df_euii |>
  dplyr::mutate(
    Analysis_Prior = factor(Analysis_Prior, levels = prior_names),
    Design_Prior   = factor(Design_Prior,   levels = dprior_names)
  )

my_theme <- theme_minimal() +
  theme(legend.position = "bottom", legend.title = element_blank())

ggplot(df_euii,
       aes(x = delta, y = EUII,
           color = Analysis_Prior,
           linetype = Analysis_Prior == "Vague")) +
  geom_line(linewidth = 1.2) +
  facet_wrap(~Design_Prior, nrow = 1) +
  geom_hline(yintercept = 1, linetype = "dashed",
             color = "black", linewidth = 0.6) +
  scale_linetype_manual(values = c("TRUE" = "dashed", "FALSE" = "solid"),
                        guide = "none") +
  scale_color_viridis_d(option = "turbo", direction = -1) +
  labs(x = expression(delta), y = "EUII") +
  my_theme
@


\phantomsection\label{sec:dual_criterion}
\paragraph{Dual Criterion}

We now replace the single efficacy rule with the dual criterion of \citet{gsponer_practical_2014}: a trial is declared successful if and only if
\[
  \Pr(\delta > 0 \mid \mathcal{D},\pi_A) \ge 0.95 \quad \text{and} \quad \Pr(\delta > 50 \mid \mathcal{D},\pi_A) \ge 0.50.
\]
The first condition is unchanged from the single-criterion design; the second adds a minimum-effect requirement at the MCID ($\delta_{\text{MCID}} = 50$), ensuring that declaring success is not driven purely by a marginally positive effect. Because both conditions must hold simultaneously, the dual criterion is strictly more conservative than the single-criterion rule for all parameter values.

\textbf{Conditional Operating Characteristics.}
Figure~\ref{fig:plot_dual_cond} shows the conditional Type I Error and conditional Power at $\delta = 60$ as functions of $\theta_C^*$.
Compared to Figure~\ref{fig:plot_mix_cond}, the overall pattern is qualitatively similar but shifted downward.
The Type I Error is uniformly lower: requiring $\Pr(\delta > 50 \mid \mathcal{D}, \pi_A) \ge 0.50$ on top of $\Pr(\delta > 0 \mid \mathcal{D}, \pi_A) \ge 0.95$ makes it harder to declare success under the null.
As already mentioned in Section~\ref{sec:dual_criterion}, power at the DV (which is $\delta = 50$ in this case), is approximately 50\% since there is an equal chance that posterior median lies on either side of the DV. The right plot 
exhibit this ehavior in the frequentist case (Vague prior), but also when the True Control Mean approximately equals the mean of the MAP prior (for all degrees of uninformativness). 



<<plot_dual_cond, echo=FALSE, warning=FALSE, message=FALSE, fig.height=4, fig.width=10, dev='cairo_pdf', fig.cap="Dual-criterion fixed design: conditional Type I Error ($\\delta = 0$, left) and conditional Power at $\\delta = 60$ (right) as functions of the fixed true control mean $\\theta_C^*$, for all six analysis priors. The dashed horizontal line marks $\\alpha = 0.05$. The Vague prior (dashed line) is the frequentist reference. The dotted vertical line marks the prior mean $\\mu_{A,C} = -50$.">>=
library(ggplot2)
library(dplyr)
library(gridExtra)

res_dual_cond <- readRDS("../Output/DUAL_cond.RDS")
df_dual_cond  <- res_dual_cond$df_cond
prior_names   <- c("MAP", "Robust (w=0.2)", "Robust (w=0.4)",
                   "Robust (w=0.6)", "Robust (w=0.8)", "Vague")
df_dual_cond$Prior <- factor(df_dual_cond$Prior, levels = prior_names)
mu_A <- -50

my_theme <- theme_minimal() +
  theme(legend.position = "bottom", legend.title = element_blank())

p_d_cond_T1E <- ggplot(df_dual_cond,
    aes(x = theta_C, y = T1E, color = Prior, linetype = Prior == "Vague")) +
  geom_line(linewidth = 1.2) +
  geom_hline(yintercept = 0.05, linetype = "dashed",
             color = "black", linewidth = 0.6) +
  geom_vline(xintercept = mu_A, linetype = "dotted",
             color = "gray", linewidth = 0.8) +
  scale_linetype_manual(values = c("TRUE" = "dashed", "FALSE" = "solid"),
                        guide = "none") +
  scale_color_viridis_d(option = "turbo", direction = -1) +
  labs(x = expression("True Control Mean (" * theta[C]^"*" * ")"),
       y = "Conditional Type I Error") +
  my_theme

p_d_cond_Power <- ggplot(df_dual_cond,
    aes(x = theta_C, y = Power, color = Prior, linetype = Prior == "Vague")) +
  geom_line(linewidth = 1.2) +
  geom_vline(xintercept = mu_A, linetype = "dotted",
             color = "gray", linewidth = 0.8) +
  scale_linetype_manual(values = c("TRUE" = "dashed", "FALSE" = "solid"),
                        guide = "none") +
  scale_color_viridis_d(option = "turbo", direction = -1) +
  labs(x = expression("True Control Mean (" * theta[C]^"*" * ")"),
       y = bquote("Conditional Power (" * delta * " = 60)")) +
  my_theme

grid.arrange(p_d_cond_T1E, p_d_cond_Power, ncol = 2)
@

\textbf{Average Metrics.}
Figure~\ref{fig:plot_dual_avg} shows the average Type I Error and average Power at $\delta = 60$ across analysis- and design-prior combinations.
The qualitative structure mirrors the single-criterion results: avgT1E inflates under skeptical design priors and is suppressed under misspecified ones, while  the MAP analysis prior aligned with the MAP design prior recovers a smaller T1E than nominal.
The dual criterion achieves substantially lower average Type I Error across all combinations, at the cost of moderate reductions in average Power.
This trade-off provides stronger protection against false positives without requiring a reduction in sample size, by instead raising the evidential bar.

<<plot_dual_avg, echo=FALSE, warning=FALSE, message=FALSE, fig.height=4, fig.width=10, dev='cairo_pdf', fig.cap="Dual-criterion fixed design: average Type I Error (left) and average Power at $\\delta = 60$ (right) for all 24 analysis$\\times$design prior combinations. The dashed horizontal line marks $\\alpha = 0.05$.">>=
library(ggplot2)
library(dplyr)
library(gridExtra)

res_dual_avg  <- readRDS("../Output/DUAL_avg.RDS")
prior_names   <- c("MAP", "Robust (w=0.2)", "Robust (w=0.4)",
                   "Robust (w=0.6)", "Robust (w=0.8)", "Vague")
dprior_names  <- c("Dirac (-50)", "MAP", "Skeptical (-90)", "Misspecified (-10)")
df_dual_avg   <- res_dual_avg$df_avg |>
  dplyr::mutate(
    Analysis_Prior = factor(Analysis_Prior, levels = prior_names),
    Design_Prior   = factor(Design_Prior,   levels = dprior_names)
  )

my_theme <- theme_minimal() +
  theme(legend.position = "bottom", legend.title = element_blank())

p_d_avg_T1E <- ggplot(dplyr::filter(df_dual_avg, delta == 0),
    aes(x = Design_Prior, y = avgT1E,
        color = Analysis_Prior, group = Analysis_Prior,
        linetype = Analysis_Prior == "Vague")) +
  geom_point(size = 2.5) +
  geom_line(linewidth = 1.0) +
  geom_hline(yintercept = 0.05, linetype = "dashed",
             color = "black", linewidth = 0.6) +
  scale_linetype_manual(values = c("TRUE" = "dashed", "FALSE" = "solid"),
                        guide = "none") +
  scale_color_viridis_d(option = "turbo", direction = -1) +
  labs(x = "Design Prior", y = "Average Type I Error") +
  my_theme

p_d_avg_Power <- ggplot(dplyr::filter(df_dual_avg, delta == 60),
    aes(x = Design_Prior, y = avgPower,
        color = Analysis_Prior, group = Analysis_Prior,
        linetype = Analysis_Prior == "Vague")) +
  geom_point(size = 2.5) +
  geom_line(linewidth = 1.0) +
  scale_linetype_manual(values = c("TRUE" = "dashed", "FALSE" = "solid"),
                        guide = "none") +
  scale_color_viridis_d(option = "turbo", direction = -1) +
  labs(x = "Design Prior",
       y = bquote("Average Power (" * delta * " = 60)")) +
  my_theme

grid.arrange(p_d_avg_T1E, p_d_avg_Power, ncol = 2)
@

\textbf{Average Power across $\delta$.}
Figure~\ref{fig:plot_dual_power_delta} shows how the average Power varies as $\delta$ increases. At the threshold $\delta = 50$ (the MCID), power is approximately $50\%$ because the second condition $\Pr(\delta > 50 \mid \mathcal{D}) \ge 0.50$ is satisfied only half the time when the true effect sits exactly at the boundary. We therefore evaluate power at $\delta = 60$, comfortably above the MCID, where the relevance condition is more easily satisfied and power rises substantially.
For $\delta < 50$, power is negligible by construction, since it will always be below 50\%.

<<plot_dual_power_delta, echo=FALSE, warning=FALSE, message=FALSE, fig.height=4, fig.width=12, dev='cairo_pdf', fig.cap="Dual-criterion fixed design: average Power as a function of the true treatment effect $\\delta$, faceted by design prior and coloured by analysis prior. The vertical dashed line marks the MCID ($\\delta = 50$), below which success declarations are almost impossible under the dual criterion. Power rises steeply beyond $\\delta = 50$ as the minimum-effect condition becomes easily satisfied.">>=
library(ggplot2)
library(dplyr)

res_dual_avg  <- readRDS("../Output/DUAL_avg.RDS")
prior_names   <- c("MAP", "Robust (w=0.2)", "Robust (w=0.4)",
                   "Robust (w=0.6)", "Robust (w=0.8)", "Vague")
dprior_names  <- c("Dirac (-50)", "MAP", "Skeptical (-90)", "Misspecified (-10)")
df_dual_avg   <- res_dual_avg$df_avg |>
  dplyr::mutate(
    Analysis_Prior = factor(Analysis_Prior, levels = prior_names),
    Design_Prior   = factor(Design_Prior,   levels = dprior_names)
  )

my_theme <- theme_minimal() +
  theme(legend.position = "bottom", legend.title = element_blank())

ggplot(df_dual_avg,
       aes(x = delta, y = avgPower,
           color = Analysis_Prior,
           linetype = Analysis_Prior == "Vague")) +
  geom_line(linewidth = 1.2) +
  geom_hline(yintercept = 0.5, linetype = "dashed", linewidth = 0.4) +
  facet_wrap(~Design_Prior, nrow = 1) +
  geom_vline(xintercept = 50, linetype = "dashed",
             color = "black", linewidth = 0.6) +
  scale_linetype_manual(values = c("TRUE" = "dashed", "FALSE" = "solid"),
                        guide = "none") +
  scale_color_viridis_d(option = "turbo", direction = -1) +
  labs(x = expression("True Treatment Effect (" * delta * ")"),
       y = "Average Power") +
  my_theme
@

\textbf{EUII and Comparison with Single Criterion.}
To assess whether the additional evidential requirement of the dual criterion translates into greater per-patient efficiency, Figure~\ref{fig:plot_dual_euii} plots the ratio of the EUII obtained using only significance threshold $\text{EUII}_{\text{Dual}}$ vs using the Dual Criterion $\text{EUII}_{\text{Sign}}$ 
at $\delta = 60$ as a function of $\omega$ (left) and across all $\delta$ values (right).
A ratio above $1$ indicates that the dual criterion extracts more information per patient enrolled; below $1$ indicates a net loss.

Under the aligned and Dirac design priors, scenarios where the prior correctly describes the truth, the ratio is close to $1$ or slightly above for informative analysis priors, meaning that the additional condition does not materially hurt per-patient efficiency.
Under the skeptical design prior, where the prior over-estimates the treatment effect, the ratio tends to fall below $1$: both criteria are harder to satisfy and power drops considerably, so the DOR shrinks.
Under the misspecified design prior, the ratio can exceed $1$ because the dual criterion's minimum-effect clause prevents inflated DOR driven by a suppressed T1E from masking poor power.
Overall, the dual criterion represents a principled stricter decision rule: it does not improve efficiency in the best case, but it limits the scope for spurious efficiency gains in adverse scenarios.

<<plot_dual_euii, echo=FALSE, warning=FALSE, message=FALSE, fig.height=4, fig.width=12, dev='cairo_pdf', fig.cap="Ratio $\\text{EUII}_{\\text{Dual}} / \\text{EUII}_{\\text{Sign}}$ comparing the dual-criterion and single-criterion fixed designs. Left: ratio at $\\delta = 60$ as a function of the robustness weight $\\omega$, one line per design prior. Right: ratio as a function of $\\delta$, faceted by design prior. The dashed horizontal line marks a ratio of $1$ (no difference between criteria).">>=
library(ggplot2)
library(dplyr)
library(gridExtra)

res_ratio    <- readRDS("../Output/DUAL_vs_FIXED_euii.RDS")
prior_names  <- c("MAP", "Robust (w=0.2)", "Robust (w=0.4)",
                  "Robust (w=0.6)", "Robust (w=0.8)", "Vague")
dprior_names <- c("Dirac (-50)", "MAP", "Skeptical (-90)", "Misspecified (-10)")
df_ratio     <- res_ratio$df_ratio |>
  dplyr::mutate(
    Analysis_Prior = factor(Analysis_Prior, levels = prior_names),
    Design_Prior   = factor(Design_Prior,   levels = dprior_names)
  )

my_theme <- theme_minimal() +
  theme(legend.position = "bottom", legend.title = element_blank())

p_ratio_omega <- ggplot(dplyr::filter(df_ratio, delta == 60),
    aes(x = omega, y = EUII_ratio,
        color = Design_Prior, group = Design_Prior)) +
  geom_line(linewidth = 1.2) +
  geom_point(size = 2.5) +
  geom_hline(yintercept = 1, linetype = "dashed",
             color = "black", linewidth = 0.6) +
  scale_x_continuous(breaks = seq(0, 1, 0.2)) +
  scale_color_viridis_d(option = "turbo", direction = -1) +
  labs(x = expression("Robustness weight (" * omega * ")"),
       y = bquote("EUII"["Dual"] / "EUII"["Sign"] ~ "(" * delta * " = 60)")) +
  my_theme

p_ratio_delta <- ggplot(df_ratio,
    aes(x = delta, y = EUII_ratio,
        color = Analysis_Prior,
        linetype = Analysis_Prior == "Vague")) +
  geom_line(linewidth = 1.2) +
  facet_wrap(~Design_Prior, nrow = 1) +
  geom_hline(yintercept = 1, linetype = "dashed",
             color = "black", linewidth = 0.6) +
  scale_linetype_manual(values = c("TRUE" = "dashed", "FALSE" = "solid"),
                        guide = "none") +
  scale_color_viridis_d(option = "turbo", direction = -1) +
  labs(x = expression(delta),
       y = bquote("EUII"["Dual"] / "EUII"["Sign"])) +
  my_theme

grid.arrange(p_ratio_omega, p_ratio_delta, ncol = 2)
@


